{"name":"Cork Bark","hex":"#7e6b43","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cork","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The dead protective tissue between the bark and cambium in woody plants, with suberin deposits making it impervious to gasses and water.","The phellem of the cork oak, used for making bottle stoppers, flotation devices, and insulation material."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To seal or stop up, especially with a cork stopper.","To blacken (as) with a burnt cork."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cork"},{"term":"bark","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make a short, loud, explosive noise with the vocal organs (said of animals, especially dogs).","To make a clamor; to make importunate outcries."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The short, loud, explosive sound uttered by a dog, a fox, and some other animals.","An abrupt loud vocal utterance."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bark"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bark (botany)","blocks":[{"text":"Bark is the outermost layer of stems and roots of woody plants. Plants with bark include trees, woody vines, and shrubs. Bark refers to all the tissues outside the vascular cambium and is a nontechnical term. It overlays the wood and consists of the inner bark and the outer bark. The inner bark, which in older stems is living tissue, includes the innermost layer of the periderm. The outer bark on older stems includes the dead tissue on the surface of the stems, along with parts of the outermost periderm and all the tissues on the outer side of the periderm. The outer bark on trees which lies external to the living periderm is also called the rhytidome."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/%E9%BB%91%E6%9D%BE_Pinus_thunbergii_20211007185113_01.jpg/330px-%E9%BB%91%E6%9D%BE_Pinus_thunbergii_20211007185113_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bark_(botany)","wikidata":"Q184453"},{"title":"Cork (material)","blocks":[{"text":"Cork is an impermeable buoyant material. It is the phellem layer of bark tissue which is harvested for commercial use primarily from Quercus suber, which is native to southwest Europe and northwest Africa. Cork is composed of suberin, a hydrophobic substance. Because of its impermeable, buoyant, elastic, and fire retardant properties, it is used in a variety of products, the most common of which is wine stoppers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Cork.jpg/330px-Cork.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cork_(material)","wikidata":"Q49444"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Poskozena_borka_na_tresni_20210821_150436.jpg/500px-Poskozena_borka_na_tresni_20210821_150436.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Poskozena_borka_na_tresni_20210821_150436.jpg/1280px-Poskozena_borka_na_tresni_20210821_150436.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Poskozena_borka_na_tresni_20210821_150436.jpg/500px-Poskozena_borka_na_tresni_20210821_150436.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Damaged bark of a cherry tree","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APoskozena_borka_na_tresni_20210821_150436.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/BarkAndWire-0308.jpg/500px-BarkAndWire-0308.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/BarkAndWire-0308.jpg/1280px-BarkAndWire-0308.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/co